{"name":"Purple Chalk","hex":"#c4adc9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"purple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour between red and blue; violet, though often closer to magenta.","Any non-spectral colour on the line of purples on a colour chromaticity diagram or a colour wheel between violet and red."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a purple hue.","Not predominantly red or blue, but having a mixture of Democrat and Republican support."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/purple"},{"term":"chalk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A soft, white, powdery limestone (calcium carbonate, CaCO3).","A piece of chalk, or nowadays processed compressed gypsum (calcium sulfate, CaSO4), that is used for drawing and for writing on a blackboard (chalkboard)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To apply chalk to anything, such as the tip of a billiard cue.","To record something, as on a blackboard, using chalk."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chalk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chalk","blocks":[{"text":"Chalk is a soft, white, porous, sedimentary carbonate rock. It is a form of limestone composed of the mineral calcite and originally formed under the sea by the accumulation and lithification of hard parts of organisms, mostly microscopic plankton, which had settled to the sea floor. Chalk is common throughout Western Europe, where deposits underlie parts of France, and steep cliffs are often seen where they meet the sea in places such as the Dover cliffs on the Kent coast of the English Channel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Seven_Sisters_3.jpg/330px-Seven_Sisters_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chalk"},{"title":"Purple","blocks":[{"text":"Purple is a color similar in appearance to violet light. In the RYB color model historically used in the arts, purple is a secondary color created by combining red and blue pigments. In the CMYK color model used in modern printing, purple is made by combining magenta pigment with cyan pigment, black pigment, or both. In the RGB color model used in computer and television screens, purple is created by mixing red and blue light in order to create colors that appear similar to violet light. According to color theory, purple is considered a cool color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Nitzana_chalk_curves_%282%29%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg/500px-Nitzana_chalk_curves_%282%29%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Nitzana_chalk_curves_%282%29%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg/500px-Nitzana_chalk_curves_%282%29%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Nitzana Chalk curves\" situated at Western Negev, Israel, are chalk deposits formed in the Mesozoic era's Tethys Ocean","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANitzana_chalk_curves_(2)%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG/500px-Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG/1280px-Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG/500px-Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG?utm_so